@meta
id: 19-03
title: Next hit, previous hit
chapter: 19
type: lesson
skills: quickfix-nav
par: 105
minutes: 6
vscode: click each result in turn
vimrc: set hidden
open: README.md

@brief
Every provider throws the same generic `NOT_FOUND` code. Support want to
know which thing was not found, so each provider gets its own:
`CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND` in the customer provider, `ORDER_NOT_FOUND` in the
order provider, `PAYMENT_NOT_FOUND` in the payment provider. The order
provider's test asserts on the code and changes to `ORDER_NOT_FOUND`
with it.

Four hits, each needing a different edit, so no single replace will do.
This is what stepping through the list is for. `:cn` goes to the next
hit and `:cp` to the previous, without opening the list at all.
`:cfirst` and `:clast` go to the ends, and `:cc 3` goes straight to hit
number three. The message line tells you where you are: `(2 of 4)`.

Write each file before you leave it. `:w` then `:cn` is the rhythm.

@keys
| `:cn` `:cnext` | next hit |
| `:cp` `:cprev` | previous hit |
| `:cfirst` `:clast` | first and last hit |
| `:cc N` | hit number N |
| `:cc` | show the current hit again |

@hints
`:vimgrep /NOT_FOUND/ **/*.ts` lands you on the first hit, in the
customer provider, with the cursor on the N.
--
`i`, the prefix, `Esc`, `:w`, `:cn`. Four times, with a different prefix
each time. The hits come in file order: customer, then the order test,
then the order provider, then payment.
--
When the message says `(4 of 4)` you are on the last one. `:wq` after
that edit.

@solution
    :vimgrep /NOT_FOUND/ **/*.ts<Enter>
    i CUSTOMER_ <Esc> :w<Enter> :cn<Enter>
    i ORDER_ <Esc> :w<Enter> :cn<Enter>
    i ORDER_ <Esc> :w<Enter> :cn<Enter>
    i PAYMENT_ <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`:cn` is the workhorse. It moves to the next entry, loads the file if
it has to, and puts the cursor on the exact column of the match, which
is why each edit here is a bare `i`: the cursor is already where the
prefix goes. You did not navigate at all; the search did.

`:cp` goes back when you overshoot. `:cc 3` is for when you have read
the list and know which entry you want. `:cc` on its own re-shows the
current entry, useful after wandering off. `:cfirst` and `:clast` are
the ends. All of them work whether or not the quickfix window is open.

Uppercase in the pattern matters here. Your vimrc sets `smartcase`, so
`/NOT_FOUND/` is a case-sensitive search and would not also match a
lowercase `not_found` somewhere. That was chapter 12; it applies to
`:vimgrep` exactly as it does to `/`.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Cancelling an order refunds it through `PaymentService.sendRefund`, which
is named after the gateway's `/sendRefund` endpoint. A refund the gateway
rejects raises `REFUND_FAILED`. Refund events go out on the
`payment.sendRefund` routing key, which the ledger service consumes.

@start src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  placedAt: Date;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.sendRefund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

